Z||000015||shizon'00||10-30-2001||08:49 PM||||There are several vettes in the 9's that are street legal.  MTI Z07's run 9's, Lingenfelter Stage II TT's run 9's, Hennessey Z600 packages will run 9's (has the power, but hasn't been around very long), but they haven't done it yet.  It only takes $4000 to have a heads/cam package installed in an LS1 car to run 11's NA!!||||reg||1
Z||000016||shizon'00||10-30-2001||08:54 PM||||Forgot about the vtec thing.  The new trailblazer I6 turns off a valves in the 6th cylinder to save fuel I believe.<P>I know GM has developed a pnuematic valve train (no camshafts).  Tell them to try to compete with that.<P>Ferrari uses a really cool idea about cams.  They have a cam with slant cut lobes.  As the rmps raise, it pushes the camshaft further into the valley to get a more aggressive lift.  It's pretty cool.  I don't know if it's in their current engines or not (don't think it is), but I know they have it.<P>Vtec sucks, all it is is a way to make "performance" engines save gas.  It doesn't add power, just provides a mild cam for lower rpm's to save fuel.  There are better ways to do it.  If they had the vtec cam in all the time, their fuel economy would suffer and it probably wouldn't idle well.<P>Tell them that.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Pontiatic||10-30-2001||09:01 PM||||Hey 88 Fiero i heard that same thing (about the trucks that save fuel) i think its kinda like the new hybrid cars in that when it is sitting in idle the engine cuts off until the noise pedal is pushed and the truck starts again. supposedly like 18% fuel efficiency increase i believe.||||reg||10
